Can a single AI inference request serve a customer and compete for a block? 

@prlnet  and @Tensorcash  are taking two different paths to bring PoW into AI data centers.

pearl-2:native  reuses matrix multiplications inside the model. In the current FP8 testnet build, that happens mainly during prefill, when the model processes the prompt.

tensorcash:native  mines during decode. As the model generates an answer, an eligible window of output tokens can be used to produce a mining proof.

With Pearl’s FP8 upgrade on testnet, I wanted to put two benchmarks side by side.

@Kairos_Res ran Llama 3.1 8B on an H100. Turning on mining reduced inference throughput by 18–41% compared with the same Pearl setup with mining off. 
https://t.co/wHCvsOP8gD

@boblabs_eu ran Qwen3.8-27B BF16 on an H100 PCIe. The model has about 3.4× as many parameters. Estimated GPU time for an 85-request coding session increased by 0.2%, and measured output throughput fell by 0.5% with four concurrent requests. 
https://t.co/Ye45bE5N72

The setups and metrics differ, and Pearl’s numbers come from an early FP8 testnet build. I’m looking forward to seeing how much overhead the team can shave off in later builds.

For a data center, the question is simple. At the same latency target, how much paid inference can each GPU-hour still deliver, and do mining rewards cover the extra cost?

@Kairos_Res, would you be up for running the same benchmark on tensorcash:native? Matching the GPU, input and output lengths, concurrency, and model where both stacks support it would make for a useful comparison. I’d love to see throughput and latency with mining on and off.

PoUW is still early. I expect several approaches to find their place, and I’m curious which inference workloads and data centers each one works best for.

